# Deep sections for the system-design extra topics (keyed by EXTRA id)

EXTRAS = {

'search': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "A search engine is two systems: an **indexing pipeline** that turns documents into an inverted index (term to list of document IDs), and a **query path** that looks up the terms, combines the lists and ranks the results. Most of the design questions are about freshness (how fast a new document becomes searchable) and fan-out (how many shards each query touches).",
  {'h': 'Ranking in two stages'},
  {'ol': ['**Retrieve** a few thousand candidates cheaply from the index: documents matching the terms, scored with BM25 or a similar text formula.', '**Re-rank** the top few hundred with richer signals: freshness, popularity, personalisation, a learned model.', 'Return the top 10 to 20, with snippets highlighted from stored text.']},
  {'h': 'Sharding choices'},
  {'table': {'head': ['Shard by', 'Pros', 'Cons'], 'rows': [
    ['Document (each shard holds a full index for its documents)', 'Simple indexing; shards independent', 'Every query hits every shard; tail latency grows with shard count'],
    ['Term (each shard holds some terms’ lists)', 'A query touches only shards for its terms', 'Hot terms overload shards; multi-term queries need cross-shard joins'],
    ['Time (recent vs old)', 'Fresh data in a small, fast, in-memory index', 'Queries over all time must merge both']]}},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Twitter', 'when': '2012 and 2020', 'title': 'Earlybird: real-time search',
    'body': ["Twitter's 2012 paper described Earlybird, the retrieval engine behind real-time search, built on Lucene but with index structures designed to ingest tweets and make them searchable almost immediately while serving queries concurrently. Tweets are partitioned across many replicated Earlybird servers, each holding in-memory segments with only the newest segment accepting writes. In 2020 Twitter wrote that it had reduced indexing latency from about 15 seconds to around one second, partly by allowing fields that arrive late (such as resolved URLs) to be added after the tweet is first indexed."],
    'lesson': 'Freshness is a design goal of its own. A small in-memory segment for new documents plus immutable older segments makes real-time search possible.',
    'src': [['Earlybird: Real-Time Search at Twitter (ICDE 2012)', 'https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~jimmylin/publications/Busch_etal_ICDE2012.pdf'], ['Reducing search indexing latency to one second', 'https://blog.x.com/engineering/en_us/topics/infrastructure/2020/reducing-search-indexing-latency-to-one-second']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// A tiny inverted index with TF-IDF ranking\nconst docs = {\n  1: 'cache invalidation is hard',\n  2: 'naming things is hard and cache naming is harder',\n  3: 'a distributed cache with consistent hashing',\n  4: 'consistent hashing moves few keys',\n};\nconst tokenize = s => s.toLowerCase().match(/[a-z]+/g);\nconst index = new Map();                                          // term -> Map(docId -> term frequency)\nfor (const [id, text] of Object.entries(docs))\n  for (const t of tokenize(text)) { if (!index.has(t)) index.set(t, new Map()); const p = index.get(t); p.set(id, (p.get(id) || 0) + 1); }\n\nfunction search(q) {\n  const N = Object.keys(docs).length, scores = new Map();\n  for (const t of tokenize(q)) {\n    const postings = index.get(t); if (!postings) continue;\n    const idf = Math.log(1 + N / postings.size);                    // rare terms count more\n    for (const [id, tf] of postings) scores.set(id, (scores.get(id) || 0) + tf * idf);\n  }\n  return [...scores].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]).map(([id, s]) => `#${id} (${s.toFixed(2)})`);\n}\nconsole.log('cache:', search('cache').join(' '));\nconsole.log('consistent cache:', search('consistent cache').join(' '));", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: indexing and ranking in 20 lines'},
  {'check': ['Separate the indexing pipeline from the query path.', 'Decide the freshness target and design segments around it.', 'Choose document, term or time sharding and say why.', 'Retrieve cheaply, then re-rank the top candidates.', 'Handle typos and synonyms at query time.', 'Measure p99 latency across all shards, not the average.']},
],

'release': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Most outages are caused by changes. Safe releasing is about making each change small, observable and quickly reversible: ship code behind a flag, expose it to a small slice of traffic, compare metrics against the rest, and widen or roll back automatically.",
  {'h': 'A rollout plan you can say in an interview'},
  {'ol': ['Deploy the new version with the feature flag off (dark launch).', 'Enable for internal users, then 1% of traffic, chosen by a stable hash of the user ID.', 'Compare error rate, latency and business metrics between flag on and off for 15 to 60 minutes.', 'Step up to 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%, with an automatic halt if metrics regress.', 'Remove the flag and dead code within a few weeks.']},
  {'h': 'Expand and contract for schema changes'},
  "Never change a column in one step. **Expand:** add the new column (nullable), deploy code that writes both, backfill old rows in batches. **Migrate:** switch reads to the new column behind a flag. **Contract:** stop writing the old column, then drop it in a later release. Each step can be rolled back on its own.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Knight Capital', 'when': 'August 2012', 'title': '$460 million lost in 45 minutes',
    'body': ["The US SEC found that Knight Capital deployed new trading code to only seven of its eight servers. The new code reused an old feature flag; on the eighth server, the flag activated long-dead code that sent millions of orders into the market. In about 45 minutes Knight accumulated losses of around $460 million. The SEC also noted there were no adequate written deployment procedures or automated checks that all servers were running the same version."],
    'lesson': 'Never reuse flags, verify every instance runs the intended version, delete dead code, and have a fast kill switch.',
    'src': [['SEC charges Knight Capital with violations of market access rule', 'https://www.sec.gov/news/press-release/2013-222']]}},
  {'case': {'who': 'GitHub', 'when': '2016', 'title': 'gh-ost: online schema migrations without triggers',
    'body': ["GitHub changes MySQL schemas many times a day. Existing online migration tools used triggers to copy live changes to a shadow table, which added load and could cause outages. GitHub built gh-ost, which copies rows to a ghost table while reading ongoing changes from the binary log as if it were a replica, can be throttled or paused at any time, and swaps tables only when they are in sync."],
    'lesson': 'Make migrations pausable and observable, and keep them from competing with production traffic.',
    'src': [['gh-ost: GitHub’s online schema migration tool for MySQL', 'https://github.blog/engineering/infrastructure/gh-ost-github-s-online-migration-tool-for-mysql/']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Stable percentage rollout: the same user always gets the same answer\nfunction bucket(key) {                       // FNV-1a hash -> 0..99\n  let h = 0x811c9dc5;\n  for (const c of key) { h ^= c.charCodeAt(0); h = Math.imul(h, 0x01000193) >>> 0; }\n  return h % 100;\n}\nconst enabled = (flag, userId, percent) => bucket(`${flag}:${userId}`) < percent;\n\nconst users = Array.from({ length: 100_000 }, (_, i) => `user-${i}`);\nfor (const pct of [1, 5, 25]) {\n  const on = users.filter(u => enabled('new-checkout', u, pct)).length;\n  console.log(`${pct}% rollout -> ${(on / 1000).toFixed(2)}% of users`);\n}\nconst stillOn = users.filter(u => enabled('new-checkout', u, 5)).every(u => enabled('new-checkout', u, 25));\nconsole.log('everyone in the 5% stays in at 25%:', stillOn);", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: rollouts that only ever widen'},
  {'check': ['Put risky changes behind flags with an owner and an expiry date.', 'Roll out by stable hash, with automatic halt on metric regression.', 'Verify version consistency across all instances after deploy.', 'Use expand and contract for every schema change.', 'Backfill in throttled batches.', 'Practise rollback; time how long it takes.']},
],

'multiregion': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Multi-region design starts with two numbers from the business: **RPO** (how much data loss is acceptable) and **RTO** (how long recovery may take). Everything else, from replication mode to how many regions run live traffic, follows from them, and each step toward zero costs more.",
  {'h': 'Patterns by cost'},
  {'table': {'head': ['Pattern', 'RPO', 'RTO', 'Cost'], 'rows': [
    ['Backups in another region', 'Hours', 'Hours to days', 'Low'],
    ['Pilot light (data replicated, minimal compute)', 'Seconds to minutes', 'Tens of minutes', 'Moderate'],
    ['Warm standby (scaled-down full stack)', 'Seconds', 'Minutes', 'Higher'],
    ['Active-active (all regions serve)', 'Near zero for region-local data', 'Seconds (routing change)', 'Highest, plus conflict handling']]}},
  {'h': 'Hidden single-region dependencies'},
  "The most common multi-region failure is a dependency that was never duplicated: an identity provider, a control plane, a DNS setup, a secrets store or a deployment tool that lives in one region. Failover drills find these; diagrams do not.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'AWS', 'when': 'December 2021', 'title': 'The us-east-1 event',
    'body': ["AWS's summary of the December 7, 2021 event explains that an automated scaling activity in its internal network triggered unexpected behaviour from many clients, causing congestion between the internal network and the main AWS network in the us-east-1 region. Many services were affected for hours, and AWS's own monitoring and support contact tools were impaired. Many customers discovered dependencies on that one region they had not planned for."],
    'lesson': 'Know which of your dependencies are regional, including control planes and tooling, and test failover for real.',
    'src': [['Summary of the AWS Service Event in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region', 'https://aws.amazon.com/message/12721/']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Health-aware region routing with a home region and failover order\nconst regions = {\n  'eu-west':  { healthy: true,  latencyMs: { eu: 20, us: 90 } },\n  'us-east':  { healthy: true,  latencyMs: { eu: 85, us: 15 } },\n  'us-west':  { healthy: true,  latencyMs: { eu: 140, us: 60 } },\n};\nfunction route(userGeo, homeRegion) {\n  if (regions[homeRegion]?.healthy) return homeRegion;          // keep users near their data\n  return Object.entries(regions).filter(([, r]) => r.healthy)\n    .sort((a, b) => a[1].latencyMs[userGeo] - b[1].latencyMs[userGeo])[0]?.[0] ?? 'none';\n}\nconsole.log('EU user, home eu-west:', route('eu', 'eu-west'));\nregions['eu-west'].healthy = false;\nconsole.log('eu-west down ->', route('eu', 'eu-west'));\nregions['us-east'].healthy = false;\nconsole.log('us-east down too ->', route('eu', 'eu-west'));", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: failover in the routing layer'},
  {'check': ['Get RPO and RTO targets per data type from the business.', 'Pick the cheapest pattern that meets them.', 'List every dependency and the region it lives in.', 'Decide which data can be written in multiple regions and how conflicts resolve.', 'Run a real failover drill at least twice a year.', 'Make failover a routing change, not a deployment.']},
],

'locks': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "A distributed lock is really a **lease**: permission that expires. The danger is a process that thinks it still holds the lease after it has expired, because it paused (garbage collection, a slow disk, a VM migration) and woke up later. Timeouts alone cannot fix this; the resource being protected has to reject stale holders.",
  {'h': 'Fencing tokens'},
  "Each time the lock service grants a lease, it returns a number that always increases. The client sends that number with every write, and the storage remembers the highest number it has accepted, rejecting anything lower. A paused client that wakes up with token 33 after another client has written with token 34 is turned away.",
  {'h': 'Do you need a lock at all?'},
  {'ul': ['A unique constraint or conditional write (`UPDATE ... WHERE version = 7`) often does the job with no lock service.', 'Partitioning work so each item has one owner (by key, through a queue partition) avoids contention.', 'Locks for efficiency (avoid doing work twice) can be best-effort; locks for correctness need fencing and a consensus-backed service (etcd, ZooKeeper).']},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Martin Kleppmann', 'when': '2016', 'title': 'How to do distributed locking',
    'body': ["In a widely read analysis, Martin Kleppmann showed how a client holding a lock can pause long enough for the lease to expire, while another client acquires it, after which both write to storage. He argued that locks used for correctness need fencing tokens checked by the storage, and that algorithms relying on timing assumptions (such as Redlock) are unsafe for that purpose, while being fine when the lock is only an efficiency optimisation."],
    'lesson': 'Ask whether the lock is for efficiency or correctness. For correctness, the protected resource must check a fencing token.',
    'src': [['How to do distributed locking (Martin Kleppmann)', 'https://martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.html']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Fencing tokens: storage rejects writes from expired lease holders\nclass LockService {\n  constructor() { this.token = 0; this.holder = null; this.expires = 0; }\n  acquire(client, now, ttl) {\n    if (this.holder && now < this.expires) return null;\n    this.holder = client; this.expires = now + ttl; return ++this.token;\n  }\n}\nclass Storage {\n  constructor() { this.maxToken = 0; this.value = null; }\n  write(value, token) {\n    if (token < this.maxToken) return `rejected (token ${token} < ${this.maxToken})`;\n    this.maxToken = token; this.value = value; return `ok (token ${token})`;\n  }\n}\nconst lock = new LockService(), db = new Storage();\nconst tA = lock.acquire('A', 0, 10);            // A gets token 1, then pauses for 15s (GC)\nconst tB = lock.acquire('B', 12, 10);           // lease expired at 10; B gets token 2\nconsole.log('B writes:', db.write('from B', tB));\nconsole.log('A wakes up and writes:', db.write('from A', tA));\nconsole.log('final value:', db.value);", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: the paused client cannot corrupt data'},
  {'check': ['Decide: is this lock for efficiency or correctness?', 'Prefer conditional writes or single ownership over locks.', 'Use a consensus-backed lock service for correctness.', 'Issue fencing tokens and check them at the storage.', 'Set lease TTLs longer than normal work, and renew them.', 'Test with injected pauses and clock skew.']},
],

'payments': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Payment systems fail in the gaps between systems: the provider charged the card but your service timed out before recording it, or you recorded it but the provider's confirmation never arrived. The design goal is that every payment ends in a known state, eventually, and is never charged twice.",
  {'h': 'The payment record as a state machine'},
  {'table': {'head': ['State', 'Means', 'Next'], 'rows': [
    ['created', 'Intent recorded locally, idempotency key assigned', 'pending'],
    ['pending', 'Request sent to the provider, outcome unknown', 'succeeded, failed, or stays pending'],
    ['succeeded', 'Provider confirmed; ledger entries written', 'refunded (partial or full)'],
    ['failed', 'Provider declined; safe to retry with a new attempt', 'terminal'],
    ['refunded', 'Money returned; reversing ledger entries written', 'terminal']]}},
  "A background reconciler finds payments stuck in `pending`, asks the provider for their status (using the same idempotency key), and moves them to a final state. Daily reconciliation against the provider's settlement report catches anything else.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Airbnb', 'when': '2019', 'title': 'Orpheus: a generic idempotency framework',
    'body': ["Airbnb described how, after moving payments to a service-oriented architecture, it needed to avoid double charges to guests and double payouts to hosts across many services. It built Orpheus, an idempotency library that splits each request into phases: before the remote call (record intent, take a lease on the idempotency key), the remote call itself, and after the call (record the result). Errors are classified as retryable or not, and results are stored so retries return the same response."],
    'lesson': 'Make idempotency a shared framework, not something each team reinvents. Separate local state changes from the remote call.',
    'src': [['Avoiding Double Payments in a Distributed Payments System (Airbnb)', 'https://medium.com/airbnb-engineering/avoiding-double-payments-in-a-distributed-payments-system-2981f6b070bb']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Payment state machine: illegal transitions are impossible\nconst ALLOWED = {\n  created: ['pending'],\n  pending: ['succeeded', 'failed'],\n  succeeded: ['refunded'],\n  failed: [], refunded: [],\n};\nfunction transition(p, to, now) {\n  if (!ALLOWED[p.state].includes(to)) throw new Error(`illegal: ${p.state} -> ${to}`);\n  return { ...p, state: to, history: [...p.history, `${now}: ${p.state} -> ${to}`] };\n}\nlet pay = { id: 'pay_1', key: 'idem-123', amount: 4999, state: 'created', history: [] };\npay = transition(pay, 'pending', 't1');\n// provider call timed out; the reconciler later asks the provider about 'idem-123'\npay = transition(pay, 'succeeded', 't9');\nconsole.log(pay.history.join(' | '));\ntry { transition(pay, 'pending', 't10'); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }\ntry { transition({ ...pay, state: 'failed' }, 'succeeded', 't11'); } catch (e) { console.log(e.message); }", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: every payment has a legal history'},
  {'check': ['Record payment intent with an idempotency key before calling the provider.', 'Model payments as a state machine with allowed transitions.', 'Run a reconciler for anything stuck in pending.', 'Write double-entry ledger entries for every money movement.', 'Reconcile daily against provider settlement reports.', 'Never retry a charge without the same idempotency key.']},
],

'booking': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Booking systems combine two difficult loads: bursts of demand for a few items (concert seats, a limited sneaker drop) and the rule that the same unit must never be sold twice. The database can enforce the rule; the architecture's job is to keep the burst from reaching the database all at once.",
  {'h': 'Layers of protection for a flash sale'},
  {'ol': ['**Static and cached pages** for browsing, so only buyers reach the application.', '**A waiting room or throttle** at the edge that admits users at the rate checkout can handle.', '**Holds with expiry** on specific units (seats) or counts (stock), written with conditional updates.', '**A payment window**; expired holds return units to the pool automatically.', '**An idempotent order creation** keyed by the hold, so retries never create a second order.']},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Shopify', 'when': '2016 onwards', 'title': 'Surviving flash sales with a checkout throttle',
    'body': ["Shopify described how merchants' flash sales sent write-heavy traffic many times their normal level straight into checkout. Rather than rebuild checkout, a small team added a throttle in their Nginx load balancers, scripted in Lua, that admits checkout traffic according to available capacity and queues the rest, later improved to treat customers fairly by the order they arrived. Shopify also isolates shops into pods so one store's sale does not take down others."],
    'lesson': 'Control admission at the edge. A fair queue in front of checkout protects the database and gives buyers a predictable experience.',
    'src': [['Surviving Flashes of High-Write Traffic Using Scriptable Load Balancers (Shopify)', 'https://shopify.engineering/surviving-flashes-of-high-write-traffic-using-scriptable-load-balancers-part-i']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Stock holds with expiry: conditional decrement and automatic release\nconst item = { stock: 3, holds: new Map() };               // holdId -> expiresAt\nfunction release(now) { for (const [id, exp] of item.holds) if (exp <= now) { item.holds.delete(id); item.stock++; } }\nfunction hold(id, now, ttl = 300) {\n  release(now);\n  if (item.stock <= 0) return 'sold out';                 // like: UPDATE stock = stock - 1 WHERE stock > 0\n  item.stock--; item.holds.set(id, now + ttl); return 'held';\n}\nfunction buy(id, now) {\n  release(now);\n  if (!item.holds.has(id)) return 'hold expired';\n  item.holds.delete(id); return 'purchased';\n}\nconst log = (...a) => console.log(...a, '| stock', item.stock);\nlog('t0   alice', hold('alice', 0));\nlog('t0   bob  ', hold('bob', 0));\nlog('t0   cara ', hold('cara', 0));\nlog('t10  dan  ', hold('dan', 10));\nlog('t100 alice', buy('alice', 100));\nlog('t400 dan  ', hold('dan', 400));                       // bob and cara expired at 300\nlog('t401 bob  ', buy('bob', 401));", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: no overselling, expired holds come back'},
  {'check': ['Cache everything a buyer sees before checkout.', 'Admit users to checkout at a rate the database can sustain.', 'Use conditional updates or unique constraints for holds.', 'Expire holds and return units automatically.', 'Make order creation idempotent by hold ID.', 'Load-test with the expected burst plus bots.']},
],

'analytics': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Analytics queries scan millions of rows to compute a few numbers; product queries fetch a few rows by key. Running both on the same database makes both worse. The standard design copies data out of the product databases into a separate analytical store, with change data capture (CDC) keeping it current.",
  {'h': 'Getting data out without hurting production'},
  {'ul': ['**CDC from the database log** (Debezium, native logical replication): every insert, update and delete as an event, with no extra load from queries.', '**Events from the application** for things that are not rows (page views, clicks), sent to a log like Kafka.', '**Batch exports** for small or slow-changing tables.']},
  {'h': 'Where it lands'},
  {'table': {'head': ['Store', 'Good for'], 'rows': [
    ['Data lake (Parquet on object storage)', 'Cheap storage of everything, raw and processed; big batch jobs'],
    ['Warehouse (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift)', 'SQL analysis, dashboards, joins across sources'],
    ['Real-time OLAP (Druid, Pinot, ClickHouse)', 'Sub-second aggregations over fresh events for user-facing analytics']]}},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Uber', 'when': '2018', 'title': 'From a few databases to 100+ petabytes',
    'body': ["Uber described how its data started in a few MySQL and PostgreSQL databases, then moved to a Vertica warehouse fed by ad hoc ETL jobs, which became expensive and fragile as data grew. The second generation ingested raw data once into a Hadoop data lake and served it with Hive, Presto and Spark. To make fresh data available within minutes without rewriting whole tables, Uber built Hudi, which supports incremental upserts on the data lake."],
    'lesson': 'Ingest raw data once, separate storage from query engines, and make incremental updates possible so freshness does not require full rewrites.',
    'src': [['Uber’s Big Data Platform: 100+ Petabytes with Minute Latency', 'https://www.uber.com/blog/uber-big-data-platform/']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Applying CDC events to keep an analytical aggregate up to date\nconst events = [\n  { op: 'insert', id: 1, after: { city: 'Lisbon', total: 30 } },\n  { op: 'insert', id: 2, after: { city: 'Porto',  total: 12 } },\n  { op: 'insert', id: 3, after: { city: 'Lisbon', total: 18 } },\n  { op: 'update', id: 2, before: { city: 'Porto', total: 12 }, after: { city: 'Porto', total: 20 } },\n  { op: 'delete', id: 1, before: { city: 'Lisbon', total: 30 } },\n];\nconst revenueByCity = {};\nconst add = (row, sign) => { revenueByCity[row.city] = (revenueByCity[row.city] || 0) + sign * row.total; };\nfor (const e of events) {\n  if (e.before) add(e.before, -1);     // undo the old version\n  if (e.after) add(e.after, +1);       // apply the new one\n}\nconsole.log(revenueByCity);           // Lisbon 18, Porto 20", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: before and after images make aggregates exact'},
  {'check': ['Never run heavy analytics on the primary product database.', 'Use CDC from the database log for tables, events for behaviour.', 'Land raw data once in cheap storage; build models on top.', 'Choose warehouse or real-time OLAP by freshness and audience.', 'Document each metric’s definition in one place.', 'Monitor pipeline lag and data quality, not just job success.']},
],

'privacy': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Privacy and tenancy are both about boundaries: which customer's data a request may touch, which region it may live in, and how to make it truly disappear when it must. These are far easier to design in at the start than to bolt on, because data copies spread into caches, logs, backups, analytics and search indexes.",
  {'h': 'Deleting data everywhere'},
  {'ol': ['Keep a **data map**: every store that holds personal data, keyed how.', 'Delete or anonymise in primary stores; emit a deletion event.', 'Every derived store (search, cache, warehouse, ML features) consumes the event and deletes.', 'For backups you cannot edit, use **crypto-shredding**: encrypt each user’s data with their own key, and delete the key.', 'Record proof of deletion with timestamps for audits.']},
  {'h': 'Tenant isolation in queries'},
  "With a shared database, a single missing `WHERE tenant_id = ?` leaks one customer's data to another. Enforce tenancy below application code: row-level security in the database, a data access layer that refuses queries without a tenant, and tests that try cross-tenant access.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Meta (Facebook)', 'when': 'May 2023', 'title': 'A €1.2 billion fine for data transfers',
    'body': ["Following a binding decision by the European Data Protection Board, Ireland's Data Protection Commission fined Meta €1.2 billion and ordered it to suspend transfers of EU users' Facebook data to the US, finding that the transfers did not adequately protect the data. It was the largest GDPR fine to date."],
    'lesson': 'Where data is stored and processed is a design requirement. Plan data residency and region boundaries early.',
    'src': [['1.2 billion euro fine for Facebook as a result of EDPB binding decision', 'https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/news/2023/12-billion-euro-fine-facebook-result-edpb-binding-decision_en']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// A data access layer that makes cross-tenant queries impossible to write by accident\nconst rows = [\n  { id: 1, tenant: 'acme', title: 'Q3 plan' },\n  { id: 2, tenant: 'globex', title: 'Secret merger' },\n  { id: 3, tenant: 'acme', title: 'Hiring' },\n];\nfunction repo(ctx) {\n  if (!ctx?.tenant) throw new Error('no tenant in request context');\n  const scoped = () => rows.filter(r => r.tenant === ctx.tenant);\n  return {\n    list: () => scoped(),\n    get: id => scoped().find(r => r.id === id) ?? null,     // other tenants' ids look like 404s\n  };\n}\nconst acme = repo({ tenant: 'acme', user: 'u1' });\nconsole.log(acme.list().map(r => r.title));\nconsole.log('acme asks for #2:', acme.get(2));\ntry { repo({}).list(); } catch (e) { console.log('blocked:', e.message); }", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: tenancy enforced in one place'},
  {'check': ['Choose a tenancy model (shared, schema per tenant, database per tenant) per tier.', 'Enforce tenant scoping below business logic (row-level security or a scoped repository).', 'Maintain a data map of every store holding personal data.', 'Propagate deletions as events to every derived store.', 'Use per-user keys where backups cannot be edited.', 'Decide data residency per region before launch.']},
],

'gossip': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Gossip spreads information the way rumours do: every node periodically tells a few random others what it knows. With no coordinator, news reaches all N nodes in roughly log(N) rounds, and the system keeps working as nodes come and go. It is used for cluster membership, failure detection and spreading small pieces of metadata.",
  {'h': 'Failure detection that scales'},
  "Heartbeating to every other node costs N squared messages. SWIM-style detectors instead have each node ping one random member per interval; if there is no reply, it asks a few others to ping that member indirectly before marking it **suspect**, and only declares it **dead** if it does not refute the suspicion in time. This keeps load constant per node and avoids false alarms from a single bad link.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Cornell (Das, Gupta, Motivala)', 'when': '2002', 'title': 'SWIM: scalable membership',
    'body': ["The SWIM paper introduced a membership protocol that separates failure detection (random probing with indirect pings) from spreading membership changes (piggybacked on the probe messages, gossip-style). Load per member stays constant as the group grows, and the time to detect a failure does not depend on group size. HashiCorp's memberlist library, used by Consul and Nomad, is based on SWIM, and HashiCorp later published Lifeguard, extensions that reduce false positives when the probing node itself is slow."],
    'lesson': 'Constant per-node work and indirect confirmation make failure detection scale to thousands of nodes without false alarms.',
    'src': [['SWIM paper (PDF)', 'https://www.cs.cornell.edu/projects/Quicksilver/public_pdfs/SWIM.pdf'], ['Lifeguard: Local Health Awareness for More Accurate Failure Detection', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.00788']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// How many gossip rounds until everyone knows? (fanout 3, repeatable random)\nlet x = 42; const rand = n => { x ^= x << 13; x ^= x >>> 17; x ^= x << 5; return Math.floor((x >>> 0) / 2 ** 32 * n); };\nfunction rounds(n, fanout = 3) {\n  const knows = new Uint8Array(n); knows[0] = 1; let count = 1, r = 0;\n  while (count < n) {\n    r++;\n    const tellers = []; for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (knows[i]) tellers.push(i);\n    for (const _ of tellers) for (let f = 0; f < fanout; f++) { const peer = rand(n); if (!knows[peer]) { knows[peer] = 1; count++; } }\n  }\n  return r;\n}\nfor (const n of [100, 1_000, 10_000, 100_000]) console.log(`${String(n).padStart(6)} nodes: ${rounds(n)} rounds (log2 n = ${Math.log2(n).toFixed(1)})`);", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: 1,000x more nodes, only a few more rounds'},
  {'check': ['Use gossip for membership and small metadata, not large data.', 'Use SWIM-style probing with indirect pings for failure detection.', 'Add a suspect state before declaring nodes dead.', 'Bound message size; piggyback updates on probes.', 'Tune intervals for detection time vs network load.', 'Test with packet loss and slow nodes, not just crashes.']},
],

'microservices': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "Microservices are a way to let many teams ship independently. They cost network calls, distributed failures, data consistency work and operational overhead. The right question is not 'monolith or microservices' but 'which boundaries would let teams move faster than the cost of crossing them'.",
  {'h': 'Signs a split will pay off'},
  {'ul': ['Teams block each other on releases or merge conflicts in the same code.', 'One part needs very different scaling or reliability (video transcoding vs account settings).', 'A clear domain boundary exists with little shared data (billing vs search).', 'A part changes at a very different pace from the rest.']},
  {'h': 'Signs it will not'},
  {'ul': ['The pieces call each other on every request and share most tables.', 'One team owns all of them.', 'You have no automated deployment, tracing or on-call per service yet.']},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Amazon Prime Video', 'when': '2023', 'title': 'Back to a monolith, 90% cheaper',
    'body': ["Prime Video's team built a tool to monitor the quality of video streams as distributed components: AWS Step Functions orchestrating Lambda functions, with frames passed through S3. At scale, the orchestration hit account limits and the S3 calls for every frame were expensive. Moving all the components into a single process on containers, with data passed in memory, reduced infrastructure cost by over 90% and removed the scaling limits."],
    'lesson': 'When components exchange large data on every step, the network between them is the cost. Split along boundaries where communication is rare.',
    'src': [['Scaling up the Prime Video audio/video monitoring service and reducing costs by 90%', 'https://www.primevideotech.com/video-streaming/scaling-up-the-prime-video-audio-video-monitoring-service-and-reducing-costs-by-90']]}},
  {'case': {'who': 'Segment', 'when': '2018', 'title': 'Goodbye microservices',
    'body': ["Segment had split its event delivery into a separate service and queue for each destination, eventually over 140 services. Each had its own copy of shared libraries at different versions, load patterns varied wildly, and the operational burden grew until the team spent most of its time maintaining services rather than improving them. They merged the destinations back into a single service with a shared test suite and found development faster and operations simpler."],
    'lesson': 'Many near-identical services owned by one team multiply maintenance without adding independence.',
    'src': [['Goodbye Microservices: From 100s of problem children to 1 superstar (Segment)', 'https://segment.com/blog/goodbye-microservices/']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Strangler fig: route path by path from the monolith to new services\nconst routes = [\n  { prefix: '/api/payments', target: 'payments-svc', percent: 100 },\n  { prefix: '/api/search',   target: 'search-svc',   percent: 20 },   // migrating, 20% of traffic\n];\nfunction hash(s) { let h = 0; for (const c of s) h = (h * 31 + c.charCodeAt(0)) >>> 0; return h % 100; }\nfunction routeRequest(path, userId) {\n  const r = routes.find(r => path.startsWith(r.prefix));\n  if (r && hash(userId) < r.percent) return r.target;\n  return 'monolith';\n}\nconst users = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, i) => 'u' + i);\nfor (const p of ['/api/payments/charge', '/api/search?q=shoes', '/api/profile']) {\n  const counts = {}; for (const u of users) { const t = routeRequest(p, u); counts[t] = (counts[t] || 0) + 1; }\n  console.log(p.padEnd(22), counts);\n}", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: migrate gradually, roll back by changing a number'},
  {'check': ['Name the team bottleneck a split would remove.', 'Draw boundaries around data ownership, not technical layers.', 'Have deployment, tracing and on-call in place before splitting.', 'Migrate with a strangler fig and traffic percentages.', 'Avoid synchronous call chains longer than two or three hops.', 'Revisit: merge services that always change together.']},
],

'specialdb': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "General-purpose databases can store anything, but some data has a shape that a specialised engine handles far better: append-heavy timestamped metrics, deeply connected graphs, high-dimensional vectors, or analytical scans of a few columns over billions of rows. Choose by access pattern, then check whether your general database already has a good-enough extension (Postgres with TimescaleDB or pgvector) before adding a new system to operate.",
  {'h': 'Matching shape to engine'},
  {'table': {'head': ['Access pattern', 'Engine type', 'Why it wins'], 'rows': [
    ['Append timestamped points, query ranges and rollups', 'Time-series (InfluxDB, TimescaleDB, Prometheus)', 'Time-partitioned storage, compression, downsampling'],
    ['Many-hop relationship queries', 'Graph (Neo4j, Neptune)', 'Traversals without join explosions'],
    ['Nearest neighbours by meaning', 'Vector (pgvector, Pinecone, Milvus)', 'Approximate nearest-neighbour indexes'],
    ['Scan and aggregate few columns of huge tables', 'Columnar (ClickHouse, BigQuery)', 'Reads only needed columns, heavy compression'],
    ['Huge write volume, query by partition key', 'Wide-column (Cassandra, ScyllaDB)', 'Log-structured writes, linear scaling']]}},
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Discord', 'when': '2017 and 2023', 'title': 'Storing trillions of messages',
    'body': ["Discord moved its messages from MongoDB to Cassandra in 2017, partitioning messages by channel and time bucket. By 2022 the Cassandra cluster had grown to many nodes holding trillions of messages and suffered from hot partitions, unpredictable latency and garbage collection pauses. Discord migrated to ScyllaDB, a Cassandra-compatible database, added data services in Rust that coalesce identical concurrent requests for the same channel, and reported much lower and more stable latencies on fewer nodes."],
    'lesson': 'A wide-column store fits append-heavy, partition-keyed data, but hot partitions remain your problem: design the key and protect the database from duplicate reads.',
    'src': [['How Discord Stores Trillions of Messages', 'https://discord.com/blog/how-discord-stores-trillions-of-messages']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// Time-series downsampling: keep raw points briefly, rollups for longer\nconst raw = Array.from({ length: 3600 }, (_, s) => ({ t: s, cpu: 40 + 30 * Math.sin(s / 300) + ((s * 7919) % 11) }));\nfunction rollup(points, bucketSec) {\n  const buckets = new Map();\n  for (const p of points) {\n    const b = Math.floor(p.t / bucketSec) * bucketSec;\n    const acc = buckets.get(b) || { n: 0, sum: 0, max: -Infinity };\n    acc.n++; acc.sum += p.cpu; acc.max = Math.max(acc.max, p.cpu); buckets.set(b, acc);\n  }\n  return [...buckets].map(([t, a]) => ({ t, avg: +(a.sum / a.n).toFixed(1), max: +a.max.toFixed(1) }));\n}\nconst perMinute = rollup(raw, 60), per10Min = rollup(raw, 600);\nconsole.log(`raw points: ${raw.length}, 1-min rollups: ${perMinute.length}, 10-min rollups: ${per10Min.length}`);\nconsole.log('10-min rollups:', per10Min.map(r => `${r.t / 60}m avg ${r.avg} max ${r.max}`).join(' | '));", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: 3,600 points become 6 summaries'},
  {'check': ['Describe the access pattern before naming a database.', 'Check whether your current database has a suitable extension.', 'Count the operational cost of each new engine (backups, upgrades, on-call).', 'Design partition keys to avoid hot partitions.', 'Plan retention and downsampling for time-series data.', 'Load-test with realistic data shapes and sizes.']},
],

'concurrency': [
  {'part': 'read'},
  "A race condition happens when the result depends on the timing of operations that overlap. The classic form is read-modify-write: two workers read a balance of 100, both add 10, both write 110, and one update is lost. The same bug appears in threads, in async code, across processes and across servers sharing a database.",
  {'h': 'Fixes, from simplest'},
  {'ol': ['**Make it atomic in one place:** `UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10` instead of reading and writing in application code.', '**Optimistic concurrency:** include a version number and write only if it has not changed (`WHERE version = 7`); retry on conflict.', '**Pessimistic locking:** `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` or a mutex, held briefly.', '**Single writer:** route all changes for a key through one owner (a queue partition, an actor).']},
  {'h': 'Async code is not race-free'},
  "JavaScript runs one thing at a time, but every `await` is a point where other work can run. Code that reads state, awaits something, then writes based on what it read has exactly the same lost-update bug as threaded code.",
  {'part': 'real'},
  {'case': {'who': 'Therac-25', 'when': '1985 to 1987', 'title': 'A race condition with fatal consequences',
    'body': ["The Therac-25 radiation therapy machine was involved in several accidents in which patients received massive overdoses, some fatal. Investigations found software race conditions: if an operator edited the treatment settings quickly, within a few seconds, the machine could end up in an inconsistent state, and hardware interlocks present in earlier models had been removed in favour of software checks. It became a standard case study in software safety."],
    'lesson': 'Races hide in timing that testing rarely hits. Make shared-state changes atomic and do not rely on a single software check for safety.',
    'src': [['Therac-25 (Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Therac-25']]}},
  {'part': 'apply'},
  {'code': "// A lost update in async JavaScript, and a fix with a tiny mutex\nconst sleep = () => new Promise(r => setImmediate(r));\nlet balance = 100;\nasync function depositUnsafe(n) { const b = balance; await sleep(); balance = b + n; }\n\nclass Mutex {\n  constructor() { this.last = Promise.resolve(); }\n  run(fn) { const next = this.last.then(fn); this.last = next.catch(() => {}); return next; }\n}\nconst m = new Mutex();\nconst depositSafe = n => m.run(async () => { const b = balance; await sleep(); balance = b + n; });\n\n(async () => {\n  balance = 100; await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 10 }, () => depositUnsafe(10)));\n  console.log('unsafe: expected 200, got', balance);\n  balance = 100; await Promise.all(Array.from({ length: 10 }, () => depositSafe(10)));\n  console.log('with mutex: expected 200, got', balance);\n})();", 'lang': 'js', 'cap': 'Run it: every await is a gap where races happen'},
  {'check': ['Find read-modify-write sequences on shared state.', 'Prefer atomic updates in the database.', 'Use version numbers for optimistic concurrency where conflicts are rare.', 'Hold locks briefly and in a consistent order to avoid deadlock.', 'Remember that every await is an interleaving point.', 'Test concurrent paths with many parallel requests.']},
],

}
